    # PORTAINER ON THE TAILNET ONLY (SPEC.md §spec:tailscale,
    # §spec:portainer-service).
    #
    # Appended by render-ignition.sh when the operator set
    # KANTAINER_PORTAINER_TAILNET_ONLY=true. Existence is the whole of the
    # switch; nothing reads the contents.
    #
    # WHAT READS IT AND WHY IT IS NOT THE FIREWALL. portainer-run reads this path
    # and publishes Portainer's port on the tailnet address instead of on every
    # address the machine has. It has to be done there, at the point the port is
    # published, because a published container port is DNAT'd and forwarded
    # rather than delivered to INPUT - so it never meets a firewalld rule at all.
    # The zone in /usr/lib/firewalld/zones/kantainer.xml says as much about its
    # own 9443 entry. A firewall-shaped answer here would look like a control and
    # be none.
    #
    # THE COST, STATED WHERE THE SWITCH IS THROWN. Binding to the tailnet address
    # means Portainer cannot start until there is one. A machine whose Tailscale
    # is broken has a Portainer that will not start, and the way back in is SSH
    # or the machine's own keyboard - which is why kantainer.conf.example asks
    # the operator to set a console password alongside this.
    #
    # The containers the operator deployed are untouched either way. This is
    # Portainer's own port, not theirs.
    - path: /etc/kantainer/portainer-tailnet-only
      mode: 0644
      user:
        id: 0
      group:
        id: 0
      contents:
        inline: |
          # This file existing is what binds Portainer to this machine's
          # tailnet address alone, instead of to every address it has. Nothing
          # reads what is written here. To put Portainer back on your own
          # network as well:
          #
          #     sudo rm /etc/kantainer/portainer-tailnet-only
          #     sudo systemctl restart kantainer-portainer
